  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "In \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(\\triangle DEF\\), \\(\\angle A=\\angle D\\) and \\(AB=DE\\). Which additional condition allows the two triangles to be proved congruent by ASA?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\angle C=\\angle F\\)",
    "B": "\\(AC=DF\\)",
    "C": "\\(BC=EF\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\angle B=\\angle E\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "ASA requires two equal angle pairs and equal included sides. The endpoints of \\(AB\\) are \\(A,B\\), and those of \\(DE\\) are \\(D,E\\). Since \\(\\angle A=\\angle D\\) is given, choice D, \\(\\angle B=\\angle E\\), directly supplies the remaining angle equality and proves congruence by ASA.\n\nIf choice A is added instead, then \\(\\angle C=\\angle F\\). Together with the given \\(\\angle A=\\angle D\\), the triangle angle sum gives\n\\[\n\\angle B=180^\\circ-\\angle A-\\angle C\n=180^\\circ-\\angle D-\\angle F=\\angle E.\n\\]\nNow \\(\\angle A=\\angle D\\), \\(AB=DE\\), and \\(\\angle B=\\angle E\\) prove congruence by ASA. Thus both A and D allow an ASA proof. D is the intended direct completion, but the displayed question does not prohibit the intermediate angle-sum step.",
